    {
      "id": "dd979ae3c561632ce4039e31221bf258bcfcfb9e",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2019718299,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Do you think it's worth it to copy the SSE4.2 implementation of Chromium over? (I think the zlib license is compatible with GPL so it should be fine)\n\n----------------\n\nI did some benchmark on the SSE4.2 implementation.\n\nI run the following command\n\n```bash\ntshark -o eth.fcs:Always -o eth.check_fcs:TRUE -r \"$inputfile\" -w /dev/null -e eth.fcs_bad -T ek > /dev/null\n```\n\nwhich forces `tshark` to compute the checksum.\n\nThe result: for a 900MB input file, the command takes around 2.5 minutes to finish, within that:\n* currently (using zlib) it takes 4.28 seconds for the CRC computation in total\n* with SSE, it would take 0.82 seconds.\n\nSo the CRC32 computation time is 3% of total time, and switching to SSE4.2 would provide a 5× speedup, which results in 2.4% improvement of total time. (Result varies per `tshark` options, I think 2.4% is a reasonably optimistic estimate.)\n\nwireshark already have facility to detect SSE4.2 support so I think it should not cause much issue. AVX512 would require more work (my benchmark result shows not much gain is achieved from AVX512 however).",
          "author": {
            "id": 7982636,
            "username": "user202729",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "user202729",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/a3a60c819d7e1cb4e39958100ffe06f4e23589ae7256cb0012f9aadac9591446?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/user202729"
          },
